<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milan Aćimović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> Aćimović was a Serbian police official and politician who served as interior minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1939. After the German conquest of 1941 he headed the Commissioner Government in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, which proved unable to contain the uprising of that summer. He then served as interior minister under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-nedic">Milan Nedić</a> and was killed in 1945 while with Chetnik forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milan-acimovic.jpg" alt="Milan Aćimović" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (possible official portrait); derivative work by Begoon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milan_A%C4%87imovi%C4%87_sh_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aćimović was born on 31 May 1898 in Pinosava, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> municipality of Voždovac. He finished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> in Belgrade and took a law degree at the University of Belgrade in 1923. On 2 September 1935 he and Velibor Jonić won from the Ministry of the Interior the legalisation of the Yugoslav National Movement, Zbor. He became chief of police in Belgrade in 1938, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-stojadinovic">Milan Stojadinović</a> made him Minister of the Interior on 21 December 1938, an office he held until 5 February 1939. In April 1939 he was arrested with Stojadinović and held until August 1940.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In April 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinhard-heydrich">Reinhard Heydrich</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and told the occupiers to rely on the senior police officers Aćimović and Dragi Jovanović, with whom he had already worked. The Germans preferred Aćimović to Dimitrije Ljotić, the leader of Zbor, as a security expert without Ljotić's ideological burden. At the end of April Harald Turner and Helmuth Förster formed the Commissioner Government with Aćimović as its head and commissioner of the interior, an auxiliary body of the German military administration rather than even a puppet government. He replaced officials who were not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> or were thought anti-German, and on 13 July 1941 ordered the arrest of family members of communists in hiding.</p><p>Divided between Ljotić's supporters and Stojadinović's old allies, the government collapsed in August 1941 when Ljotić withdrew two ministers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-nedic">Milan Nedić</a> took over. Aćimović stayed on as Nedić's interior minister. He kept contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draza-mihailovic">Draža Mihailović</a>'s movement and in December 1941 warned Mihailović of an operation against him; when the Germans learned of it, Nedić barred him from the matter, and on 10 November 1942 Tanasije Dinić replaced him because of his Chetnik ties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Germans were driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in October 1944, Aćimović served as a link between the German envoy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, Hermann Neubacher, and Mihailović, travelling to Mihailović's headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. He died on 25 May 1945 in the Battle of Zelengora while retreating with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a> before the Partisans.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Milan Aćimović” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A commissioner government led by <b>Milan Aćimović</b> proved unable to cope with the uprising of summer 1941, and on 29 August 1941 the Germans installed the former war minister General Milan Nedić at the head of a Government of National Salvation.</blockquote><a href="/state/government-of-national-salvation">Government of National Salvation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pinosava, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in Zelengora, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.69,20.48&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Pinosava on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Milan Aćimović"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4424 3733h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4424 3733h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.69,20.48&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
